, published 2009, page 109:"}, {"text": "This was followed by his presenting his sable acquaintance with a fig of tobacco, whereupon, instead of thanking the donor in the usual way, the black signified his gratitude by throwing a spear at twenty or thirty yards' distance.", "reference": "1887, Harriet W. Daly, Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia, page 289:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A fico, a contemptuous gesture.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/fɪɡ/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "figs", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "figging", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "figged", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "figged", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(obsolete) To insult with a fico, a contemptuous gesture.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "When Pistol lies, do this, and fig me like / The bragging Spaniard.", "reference": "c. 1596–1599 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Second Part of Henry the Fourth, […]. Epilogue.”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act V, scene iii]:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) To put into the head of, as something useless or contemptible.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(soap-making, dated) To develop, or cause (a soap) to develop, white streaks or granulations.", "tags": ["dated"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "For filling figged soaps silicate of potash is best adapted, as soda prevents in a measure the proper crystallization. [...] Artificially figged soap [...makes] a very close imitation of the naturally figged soap.", "reference": "1893, Henry Gathmann, American Soaps, page 204:"}, {"text": "Figging is usually considered to indicate a good quality of soft soap, but such is really not the case. A first-class soft soap can be made which will not fig, while, on the other hand, a poor soap can be produced which will fig.", "reference": "1897, The National Provisioner, page 27:"}, {"text": "In the cold soaps, the water soluble color is added in liquid form after saponification has started. In figged soaps, the color is crutched in after saponification is completed.", "reference": "1938, Harry Bennett, The Standard Book of Formulas:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/fɪɡ/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "figs", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "figging", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "figged", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "figged", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(intransitive) To move suddenly or quickly; rove about.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/fɪɡ/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "figs", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "fig.", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Abbreviation of figure (“diagram or illustration”).", "tags": ["abbreviation", "alt of"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []},